  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Spotty minor tidal flooding expected with \nThursday morning's high tide. \n\nElevated astro-tides from the recent full moon and northeast \nonshore flow resulted in some spotty minor tidal flooding across\nportions of the southern New Jersey, Delaware and areas along \nthe southern Delaware Bay earlier this morning. For those same \nareas, another round of some spotty minor tidal flooding is \npossible with the Thursday morning high tide cycle. However, \ntide levels should be lower than this morning and no advisories \nare warranted at this time.\n\nNo tidal flooding is expected on the tidal Delaware River, \nnorthern New Jersey coast, northern Delaware Bay and Chesapeake\nBay communities.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Patchy fog possible tomorrow morning. Otherwise,\nno other significant weather concerns over the next several\ndays.\n\nLatest forecast guidance shows increased confidence in patchy\nfog and/or low stratus Thursday morning. Current satellite \nimagery shows high clouds across the region this evening, but\nclearer skies just behind it. Short-range guidance suggests\nhigh clouds will clear overnight with winds decoupling in time\nfor some fog/low stratus along the I-95 corridor and west. \n\nHigh pressure moves offshore tonight. A weak cold front passes \nthrough the region Friday, and then high pressure returns for \nthe weekend. Low pressure may approach the region for the start \nof next week, but odds of this are diminishing, which may result\nin the region staying dry for another week.\n\nDry conditions are expected through at least Sunday with high \ntemperatures generally from the upper 60s to low 70s on Thursday\nand Friday. Cooler, but still slightly above normal \ntemperatures expected for this weekend. The only thing of note\nis some patchy fog developing late tonight/early tomorrow\nmorning, mainly over rural areas across the region. This could \nresult in some brief visibility restrictions during the morning \ncommute, though fog is not currently expected to be widespread. \n\nThe system which had been progged to affect the region early\nnext week now appears to weaken and veer away from the region\nfor the most part, keeping our region dry. Extended guidance has\na vigorous system affecting the area near the end of next week,\nwhich now may be the next decent chance of any rain.",
